egression step at the end of the pipeline\nnames = model[0].get_feature_names_out()         # the 12 column names from the preprocessing step\n\ncoefs = pd.Series(lr.coef_, index=names)\nprint(\"intercept:\", round(lr.intercept_))\nprint()\nprint(coefs.reindex(coefs.abs().sort_values(ascending=False).index).round(0))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-06T07:46:33.03877Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-06T07:46:33.03979Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-06T07:46:33.082403Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-06T07:46:33.039751Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-06T07:46:33.079257Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"intercept: 219658\n\ncat__ocean_proximity_ISLAND        156767.0\nnum__median_income                  73856.0\nnum__latitude                      -52429.0\nnum__longitude                     -51776.0\nnum__population                    -43912.0\ncat__ocean_proximity_INLAND        -40424.0\nnum__households                     29573.0\nnum__total_bedrooms                 28699.0\nnum__housing_median_age             13520.0\nnum__total_rooms                    -9515.0\ncat__ocean_proximity_NEAR OCEAN      4601.0\ncat__ocean_proximity_NEAR BAY       -4445.0\ndtype: float64\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":15},{"cell_type":"code","source":"w_income = coefs[\"num__median_income\"]","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-06T07:46:33.084646Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-06T07:46:33.085584Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-06T07:46:33.11197Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-06T07:46:33.085531Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-06T07:46:33.10783Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":16},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# convert the income weight from \"per 1 std\" to \"per +1 unit\" (+$10k)\nincome_std = X_train[\"median_income\"].std()\nw_income = coefs[\"num__median_income\"]\n\nprint(\"income std (in $10k units):\", round(income_std, 2))\nprint(\"per 1 std of income:\", round(w_income))\nprint(\"per +$10k of income:\", round(w_income / income_std))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-06T07:46:33.114Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-06T07:46:33.11462Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-06T07:46:33.128885Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-06T07:46:33.114559Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-06T07:46:33.126793Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"income std (in $10k units): 1.91\nper 1 std of income: 73856\nper +$10k of income: 38769\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":17},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"- median_income is the strongest real signal: +73,856 per standard deviation (std = 1.91, i.e. about $19k), which works out to about +38,800 per extra $10k of income, holding everything else fixed.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### 8a. Feature engineering\n\nThe count columns (rooms, bedrooms, population, households) are district totals and strongly correlated, which made their coefficients hard to read. I add ratios that describe a typical home: rooms per household, bedrooms per room and population per household. I also add one interaction, median income × INLAND, so income can have a different slope for inland districts.\n\nThese features only use values from the same row, so computing them outside the pipeline doesn't leak anything from the validation set.\n\nNo new imports in this step.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def add_features(d):\n    d = d.copy()\n    d[\"rooms_per_household\"] = d[\"total_rooms\"] / d[\"households\"]\n    d[\"bedrooms_per_room\"] = d[\"total_bedrooms\"] / d[\"total_rooms\"]\n    d[\"population_per_household\"] = d[\"population\"] / d[\"households\"]\n    d[\"income_x_inland\"] = d[\"median_income\"] * (d[\"ocean_proximity\"] == \"INLAND\")\n    return d\n\nX_train_fe = add_features(X_train)\nX_val_fe = add_features(X_val)\n\nnum_cols_fe = X_train_fe.select_dtypes(include=\"number\").columns.tolist()\n\npreprocess_fe = ColumnTransformer([\n    (\"num\", make_pipeline(SimpleImputer(strategy=\"median\"), StandardScaler()), num_cols_fe),\n    (\"cat\", make_pipeline(SimpleImputer(strategy=\"most_frequent\"),\n                          OneHotEncoder(drop=\"first\", handle_unknown=\"ignore\")), cat_cols),\n])\n\nmodel_fe = make_pipeline(preprocess_fe, LinearRegression())\nmodel_fe.fit(X_train_fe, y_train)\npred_fe = model_fe.predict(X_val_fe)\n\nrmse_fe = np.sqrt(mean_squared_error(y_val, pred_fe))\nr2_fe = r2_score(y_val, pred_fe)\ncv_fe = -cross_val_score(model_fe, X_train_fe, y_train, cv=5,\n                         scoring=\"neg_root_mean_squared_error\")\n\nprint(\"features:\", len(num_cols_fe), \"numeric +\", len(cat_cols), \"categorical\")\nprint(\"val RMSE:\", round(rmse_fe), \"  (before:\", round(rmse_lr), \")\")\nprint(\"val R2:\", round(r2_fe, 3), \"  (before:\", round(r2_lr, 3), \")\")\nprint(\"CV RMSE:\", round(cv_fe.mean()), \"±\", round(cv_fe.std()), \"  (before:\", round(cv_scores.mean()), \")\")\nprint(\"negative predictions:\", (pred_fe < 0).sum())","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-06T07:46:33.130658Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-06T07:46:33.131476Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-06T07:46:33.718642Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-06T07:46:33.131405Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-06T07:46:33.71777Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"features: 12 numeric + 1 categorical\nval RMSE: 68629   (before: 68953 )\nval R2: 0.642   (before: 0.639 )\nCV RMSE: 68037 ± 2486   (before: 68857 )\nnegative predictions: 6\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":18},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Notes:** With the new features, validation RMSE goes from 68,953 to 68,629 and R² from 0.639 to 0.642. CV RMSE is 68,037 ± 2,486 (before: 68,857). The gain is well inside the fold-to-fold noise of about 2.5k, so I can't call it a real improvement. Negative predictions dropped from 11 to 6. The ratios are easier to interpret than the raw totals, but they don't address the main problems (the cap, non-linear location, skewed target).","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### 8b. Log target\n\nThe target is right-skewed and the linear model predicts some negative prices. Here I train on log(price) and convert predictions back with exp(), which guarantees positive predictions and makes the model fit relative (percentage) errors instead of dollar errors. I keep the features from 8a. RMSE is still measured in dollars so it can be compared with the earlier rows.\n\nNew imports:\n- `TransformedTargetRegressor` (sklearn.compose): wraps a model so the target is transformed before fitting (here `np.log`) and predictions are transformed back automatically (here `np.exp`). This keeps the log step inside the model, so cross-validation handles it correctly too.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"from sklearn.compose import TransformedTargetRegressor\n\nmodel_log = TransformedTargetRegressor(\n    regressor=make_pipeline(preprocess_fe, LinearRegression()),\n    func=np.log,\n    inverse_func=np.exp,\n)\nmodel_log.fit(X_train_fe, y_train)\npred_log = model_log.predict(X_val_fe)          # already back in dollars\n\nrmse_log = np.sqrt(mean_squared_error(y_val, pred_log))\nr2_log = r2_score(y_val, pred_log)\ncv_log = -cross_val_score(model_log, X_train_fe, y_train, cv=5,\n                          scoring=\"neg_root_mean_squared_error\")\n\nprint(\"val RMSE:\", round(rmse_log), \"  (8a:\", round(rmse_fe), \")\")\nprint(\"val R2:\", round(r2_log, 3), \"  (8a:\", round(r2_fe, 3), \")\")\nprint(\"CV RMSE:\", round(cv_log.mean()), \"±\", round(cv_log.std()), \"  (8a:\", round(cv_fe.mean()), \")\")\nprint(\"negative predictions:\", (pred_log < 0).sum())\nprint(\"lowest prediction:\", round(pred_log.min()), \"  highest:\", round(pred_log.max()))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-06T07:46:33.719636Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-06T07:46:33.72039Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-06T07:46:34.368682Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-06T07:46:33.720353Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-06T07:46:34.367867Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"val RMSE: 87046   (8a: 68629 )\nval R2: 0.425   (8a: 0.642 )\nCV RMSE: 85402 ± 4057   (8a: 68037 )\nnegative predictions: 0\nlowest prediction: 37659   highest: 1378858\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":19},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Notes:** Training on log(price) removed every negative prediction (lowest is now 37,659), but the overall score got much worse: validation RMSE 87,046 vs 68,629 and R² 0.425 vs 0.642. CV agrees (85,402 ± 4,057), so it isn't a bad split.\n\nWhy I think it got worse:\n- exp() keeps predictions positive but amplifies overshoots at the top end. The highest prediction is 1,378,858, while nothing in the data goes above 500,001. A few of these huge misses dominate the squared error.\n- The target is capped, so the true value for rich districts is stuck at 500,001 while the log model keeps climbing.\n- The log model minimises relative (percentage) error, but I'm scoring it on dollar RMSE, which is driven by the expensive districts. The training objective and the evaluation metric don't match.\n\nTakeaway: a log target makes sense when the metric is relative (e.g. RMSLE) or when negative predictions are unacceptable. For dollar RMSE on a capped target it hurts.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### 8c. Diagnosing the log model\n\nThree checks: how many predictions go above the 500,001 cap, whether clipping predictions to the valid range [14,999, 500,001] fixes the dollar RMSE, and how both models compare on RMSLE (RMSE of log values), which measures percentage error instead of dollar error.\n\nNo new imports in this step.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"lo, hi = 14999, 500001          # smallest and largest values in the target\n\ndef rmse(p):\n    return np.sqrt(mean_squared_error(y_val, p))\n\ndef rmsle(p):\n    return np.sqrt(np.mean((np.log(p) - np.log(y_val)) ** 2))\n\nfor name, p in [(\"8a dollar target\", pred_fe), (\"8b log target\", pred_log)]:\n    p_clip = np.clip(p, lo, hi)\n    print(name)\n    print(\"  predictions above cap:\", (p > hi).sum())\n    print(\"  RMSE raw:\", round(rmse(p)), \"  RMSE clipped:\", round(rmse(p_clip)))\n    print(\"  RMSLE (clipped):\", round(rmsle(p_clip), 3))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-06T07:46:34.36965Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-06T07:46:34.369964Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-06T07:46:34.389258Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-06T07:46:34.369932Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-06T07:46:34.388373Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"8a dollar target\n  predictions above cap: 37\n  RMSE raw: 68629   RMSE clipped: 67243\n  RMSLE (clipped): 0.35\n8b log target\n  predictions above cap: 65\n  RMSE raw: 87046   RMSE clipped: 69079\n  RMSLE (clipped): 0.313\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":20},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Notes:**\n- Predictions above the 500,001 cap: 37 for the dollar model, 65 for the log model.\n- Clipping predictions to [14,999, 500,001] helps both: dollar model 68,629 → 67,243, log model 87,046 → 69,079. Almost all of the log model's extra error came from a small number of over-cap predictions.\n- After clipping the two are within about 1.8k RMSE of each other, which is inside the CV noise.\n- On RMSLE (percentage error) the log model is better: 0.313 vs 0.350.\n- So the better model depends on the metric: dollar RMSE favours the dollar target, relative error favours the log target. Clipping to the valid range is a cheap fix I should apply whenever the target is bounded.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## What I learned\n\n- A mean baseline gives RMSE ≈ std(y) and R² ≈ 0 (114,756 here). Every later number is judged against it.\n- Plain linear regression in a leakage-safe pipeline cut RMSE by about 40 % (68,953, R² 0.639). Train and validation errors were almost equal, so the model underfits rather than overfits.\n- Most of the remaining error comes from the 500,001 cap and from location not being linear. Capped districts were 4.5 % of validation but about a quarter of the squared error.\n- Coefficients need care: a huge weight on a 5-row category (ISLAND) means little, and correlated count columns get unstable signs.\n- Ratio features and an income × inland interaction gave a gain smaller than the CV noise, so I can't claim an improvement.\n- A log target removed negative predictions and gave the best percentage error (RMSLE 0.313), but it overshot the cap and hurt dollar RMSE until predictions were clipped.\n- Clipping predictions to the valid range is a cheap fix; the dollar model with clipping was the best on RMSE (67,243).\n- Which model is \"best\" depends on the metric, so the metric has to be chosen from the business question first.\n\nNext: regularisation (module 4.2) and non-linear models should handle location and the cap better.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"probs = np.full(20000, 0.01)\nprint(np.prod(probs))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-06T07:46:34.390654Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-06T07:46:34.391476Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-06T07:46:34.42039Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-06T07:46:34.391426Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-06T07:46:34.418081Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"0.0\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":21},{"cell_type":"code","source":"print(np.sum(np.log(probs)))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-06T07:46:34.421719Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-06T07:46:34.422988Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-06T07:46:34.453536Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-06T07:46:34.422942Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-06T07:46:34.45275Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"-92103.40371976182\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":22},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### 9. Huber loss\n\nLinear regression minimises squared error, so the capped districts (large errors) pull the fit hard. Huber loss is squared for small errors and linear for large ones, which caps that pull. Here I compare the two on the same features (from 8a).\n\nNew imports:\n- `HuberRegressor` (sklearn.linear_model): a linear model trained with Huber loss. `epsilon` sets where the loss switches from squared to linear; 1.35 is the default.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"from sklearn.linear_model import HuberRegressor\nmodel_huber = make_pipeline(preprocess_fe,HuberRegressor(epsilon=1.35,max_iter=1000))\nmodel_huber.fit(X_train_fe,y_train)\npred_huber= model_huber.predict(X_val_fe)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-06T07:46:34.454555Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-06T07:46:34.456347Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-06T07:46:35.286698Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-06T07:46:34.456305Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-06T07:46:35.285903Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":23},{"cell_type":"code","source":"pred_huber","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-06T07:46:35.287707Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-06T07:46:35.288012Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-06T07:46:35.299809Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-06T07:46:35.287983Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-06T07:46:35.298565Z"}},"outputs":[{"execution_count":24,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"array([245062.46359886,  87277.59153553, 192444.3603175 , ...,\n       114352.54975576, 218251.1141195 ,  61645.59717847])"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":24},{"cell_type":"code","source":"print(pred_huber[:5].round(0))\nprint(pred_fe[:5].round(0))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-06T07:46:35.301057Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-06T07:46:35.30139Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-06T07:46:35.327909Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-0